\title{Higher-Order Co-Citation Complexes:\\Reconstruction, Filtrations, and Universal Betti Bounds}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A finite citation corpus defines a hypergraph whose vertices are mathematical theorems and whose hyperedges are the theorem sets jointly cited by individual documents. We study its downward-closed simplicial complex and compare it with the clique complex of the pairwise co-citation graph. Every genuine face becomes a graph clique, but the converse can fail because different pairs may have different witnesses. We prove that pairwise data reconstructs the full complex exactly when every clique has a common document witness. A three-vertex example shows that pairwise projection can fill a nonexistent triangle and destroy first homology. Nested corpora induce simplicial filtrations. On $n$ vertices, every $k$th Betti number is bounded by $\binom{n}{k+1}$ and hence by $n^{k+1}$, while all homology in dimensions $k\ge n$ vanishes. Thus the power is a coarse ceiling, not a universal asymptotic law. We give algorithms and distinguish structural topology from semantic claims about communities and paradigm shifts.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Citation data is usually represented by a graph, yet a document can cite several theorems simultaneously. Joint citation is therefore a higher-order relation. A graph triangle can mean either that one document cites all three vertices or that three documents separately cite the three pairs. The graph is identical in both cases, but the higher-dimensional topology differs.

The natural primitive is a finite hypergraph of citation sets. Taking its downward closure produces a simplicial complex that preserves common-document witnesses. This paper proves four structural facts. Genuine faces always become cliques in the pairwise graph. Equality with the clique complex holds exactly under a conformality condition. Adding documents can only add faces and therefore yields filtrations. Finally, face counts and Betti numbers obey universal binomial bounds and vanish above the ambient dimension.

These facts refine two tempting claims. The inequality $\beta_k\le n^{k+1}$ does not imply $\beta_k\asymp n^{k+1}$, because boundary ranks can eliminate most or all homology. Likewise, homology records incidence but does not intrinsically identify a mathematical school or a paradigm shift. Such semantics require metadata or a generative model.

Throughout, $V$ is a finite set of theorems, $n=|V|$, and homology is computed over a fixed field $\mathbb F$.

\section{Corpora, complexes, and graphs}

\begin{definition}[Co-citation corpus]
A corpus on $V$ is a finite family $C=\{W_1,\ldots,W_m\}$ with $W_i\subseteq V$, where $W_i$ is the set of theorems cited by one document.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Co-citation complex]
The complex generated by $C$ is
\[
K(C)=\{S\subseteq V:\text{ there exists }W\in C\text{ with }S\subseteq W\}.
\]
A member with $k+1$ vertices is a $k$-face.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Downward closure]
If $S\in K(C)$ and $T\subseteq S$, then $T\in K(C)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose $W\in C$ with $S\subseteq W$. Then $T\subseteq S\subseteq W$, so the same document witnesses $T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Pairwise graph and clique complex]
The graph $G(C)$ has vertex set $V$, with distinct $x,y$ adjacent exactly when some $W\in C$ contains both. For a graph $G$, its clique complex $\operatorname{Cl}(G)$ consists of all vertex sets whose distinct pairs are adjacent.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Conformality]
A corpus $C$ is conformal if every clique $S$ of $G(C)$ is contained in one member of the corpus:
\[
S\in\operatorname{Cl}(G(C))\Longrightarrow
\exists W\in C,\quad S\subseteq W.
\]
\end{definition}

Let $f_k(C)$ be the number of $k$-faces. The $k$-chain space has dimension $f_k(C)$. If $r_k$ is the rank of the simplicial boundary map $\partial_k$, then rank--nullity gives
\[
\beta_k(C)=f_k(C)-r_k-r_{k+1}.
\]
Thus face counts provide generators, while boundary ranks determine which generators survive in homology.

\section{Pairwise reconstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Genuine faces become cliques]
For every corpus $C$,
\[
K(C)\subseteq\operatorname{Cl}(G(C)).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $S\in K(C)$, some $W\in C$ contains $S$. Every two distinct vertices of $S$ occur together in $W$, so they are adjacent. Hence $S$ is a clique.
\end{proof}

The inclusion has a consequential direction: flag completion never removes a genuine face, but it may insert an unwitnessed one.

\begin{theorem}[Conformality Criterion]
For every finite corpus $C$,
\[
K(C)=\operatorname{Cl}(G(C))
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
C\text{ is conformal}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If the complexes are equal, every graph clique lies in $K(C)$ and hence is contained in a corpus member. Conversely, suppose $C$ is conformal. The preceding theorem gives one inclusion. Every face of $\operatorname{Cl}(G(C))$ is a clique, so conformality gives a common witness and places it in $K(C)$, proving the reverse inclusion.
\end{proof}

This theorem gives an exact certificate of information loss: a clique lacking a common document witness proves that the pairwise representation is insufficient.

\begin{example}[Triangle boundary]
Let $V=\{0,1,2\}$ and
\[
C_\triangle=\bigl\{\{0,1\},\{0,2\},\{1,2\}\bigr\}.
\]
Every pair occurs, so $G(C_\triangle)$ is complete and its clique complex contains the filled triangle $\{0,1,2\}$. No corpus member contains all three vertices, so that face is absent from $K(C_\triangle)$. Hence
\[
K(C_\triangle)\subsetneq\operatorname{Cl}(G(C_\triangle)).
\]
The genuine complex is a triangle boundary with $\beta_1=1$; its flag completion is a filled triangle with $\beta_1=0$. Pairwise projection followed by clique completion has erased a genuine loop.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[Universal document]
If $C=\{V\}$, then $K(C)=2^V$, the graph $G(C)$ is complete, and $C$ is conformal.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every subset of $V$ has the common witness $V$. This also witnesses every pair and every clique.
\end{proof}

A complete pairwise graph may therefore encode either a hollow boundary or a full simplex; only common witnesses distinguish them.

\section{Corpus growth and persistence}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity]
If $C\subseteq D$, then $K(C)\subseteq K(D)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every face in $K(C)$ has a witness in $C$, and that witness also belongs to $D$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Temporal filtration]
Let $(I,\leq)$ be ordered and suppose $C_s\subseteq C_t$ whenever $s\leq t$. Then
\[
K(C_s)\subseteq K(C_t),
\]
so $(K(C_t))_{t\in I}$ is a simplicial filtration.
\end{corollary}

This supports persistent homology. In the triangle example, three pair-documents can create a one-cycle; a later document citing all three inserts the triangular face and kills it. In dimension two, jointly cited triples may form a tetrahedral shell, and a later four-way citation may fill it. Persistence measures the duration of such witness gaps. It does not, without further assumptions, determine their disciplinary meaning.

\section{Universal bounds}

\begin{theorem}[Binomial face bound]
For every $q\geq0$,
\[
\bigl|\{S\in K(C):|S|=q\}\bigr|\leq\binom nq.
\]
Equivalently, $f_k(C)\leq\binom n{k+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every $q$-vertex face is one of the $\binom nq$ subsets of size $q$ in the ambient set. The corpus can select only some of them.
\end{proof}

The bound is sharp for face counts: a universal document produces the full simplex and attains equality in every available dimension.

\begin{theorem}[Universal Betti ceiling]
For every $k\geq0$,
\[
\beta_k(C)\leq f_k(C)\leq\binom n{k+1}\leq n^{k+1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Homology is a quotient of the cycle space, which is a subspace of the $k$-chain space, so $\beta_k\leq f_k$. Apply the binomial face bound. Finally, every $(k+1)$-element subset can be represented by an ordered $(k+1)$-tuple, and there are $n^{k+1}$ such tuples.
\end{proof}

For fixed $k$,
\[
\binom n{k+1}\sim\frac{n^{k+1}}{(k+1)!},
\]
but even the sharper ceiling does not determine $\beta_k$. Boundary ranks may be large; a full simplex has many faces and trivial reduced homology.

\begin{theorem}[Dimension obstruction]
If $k\geq n$, then
\[
f_k(C)=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad\beta_k(C)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A $k$-simplex requires $k+1$ distinct vertices. If $k\geq n$, no such subset of an $n$-element set exists. The chain space and its homology are therefore zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No unrestricted positive power law]
If $n>0$ and $k\geq n$, then
\[
\beta_k(C)=0<n^{k+1}.
\]
Consequently, neither equality with $n^{k+1}$ nor a two-sided comparison with a positive multiple of it can hold uniformly in all dimensions.
\end{corollary}

This does not preclude model-dependent asymptotics for fixed $k$ as $n$ grows. It shows that such claims need a specified regime and assumptions controlling boundary ranks.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Constructing the genuine complex}
For each citation set $W_i$, enumerate all subsets and insert them into a set. The resulting union is $K(C)$. If $d=\max_i|W_i|$, this requires
\[
O\left(\sum_i2^{|W_i|}\right)\subseteq O(m2^d)
\]
subset generations, apart from hashing. Exponential dependence is unavoidable when the output itself has exponentially many faces.

\subsection{Testing conformality}
First connect every pair within each document to construct $G(C)$. Enumerate maximal cliques. The corpus is conformal exactly when every maximal clique lies inside some document. Testing maximal cliques suffices because each clique extends to a maximal one and a witness for the latter witnesses all its subsets. Maximal-clique enumeration is exponential in the worst case, but failure returns a useful certificate: an unwitnessed clique.

\subsection{Computing homology}
Order the faces and construct each boundary matrix $B_k$. Over $\mathbb F_2$, a column has a one at every codimension-one face of its simplex. Gaussian elimination gives $r_k$, and then
\[
\beta_k=f_k-r_k-r_{k+1}.
\]
Dense elimination is cubic in a characteristic matrix size; sparse methods are often preferable. Every result should satisfy
\[
0\leq\beta_k\leq f_k\leq\binom n{k+1}.
\]

\subsection{Temporal persistence}
Sort documents by time and assign each newly witnessed simplex its first appearance time. Downward closure guarantees that faces appear no later than their cofaces, producing a valid filtration. Boundary-matrix reduction then pairs births and deaths. This procedure should use the genuine complex unless conformality is known; otherwise flag completion may introduce spurious deaths.

\section{Interpretation and applications}
The Conformality Criterion diagnoses exactly where a graph overstates collective evidence. An interface can expose each unwitnessed clique together with the separate documents supporting its edges. Persistent homology can then track how long such higher-order gaps survive.

Persistent $H_2$ may be more informative than raw triangle counts for certain structural changes. Counts measure abundance, but homology measures assembly: the same number of triangles can form a cavity or a filled, contractible region. A long-lived class records an attachment pattern whose filling witnesses remain absent.

Nevertheless, topology alone cannot recover independently assigned semantic labels. The same abstract complex can be decorated with incompatible labels while retaining every Betti number and persistence interval. Claims about schools of mathematics therefore require vertex metadata, text, authorship, or an explicit model linking assortativity to incidence. Topology supplies structural features, not self-interpreting semantics.

The binomial ceiling motivates normalized statistics such as
\[
\frac{\beta_k}{\binom n{k+1}},
\]
when the denominator is nonzero. Their limiting behavior cannot be inferred without a corpus model specifying document sizes, theorem degrees, dependence, and whether $k$ is fixed.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The central distinction is logical. Membership in $K(C)$ asserts one common witness, whereas membership in $\operatorname{Cl}(G(C))$ asserts only a family of pairwise witnesses. Conformality is exactly the condition under which these existential witnesses can be synchronized.

Four research directions follow. First, sparse random corpus models may yield deterministic limits for normalized fixed-dimensional Betti numbers. Second, growing random corpora may exhibit a threshold at which every clique obtains a common witness. Third, semantic recovery should be studied under explicit metadata and assortativity assumptions. Fourth, persistent $H_2$ should be compared empirically with motif counts for detecting localized failures of conformality.

Empirical reports should state whether their simplices are genuinely witnessed or inserted by flag completion, report unwitnessed cliques, identify the coefficient field, and distinguish raw from normalized Betti numbers. Temporal studies should also specify the filtration parameter and treatment of simultaneous events.

\section{Conclusion}
A theorem network has more structure than its edges. The downward closure of document citation sets preserves common witnesses. Every genuine face is a pairwise clique, but the graph reconstructs all higher-order faces exactly only under conformality. The triangle boundary is the minimal obstruction and shows that flag completion can erase homology.

Nested corpora form valid filtrations. On $n$ vertices,
\[
0\leq\beta_k\leq f_k\leq\binom n{k+1}\leq n^{k+1},
\]
and $\beta_k=0$ for $k\geq n$. These exact statements replace an unsupported universal power law. They also locate the boundary between structure and interpretation: topology detects incidence patterns, while claims about communities or paradigm shifts require additional data and assumptions.

