# Explore the Shape of Shared Ideas

A citation graph remembers pairs; a co-citation complex remembers which groups share one document witness. Begin with the smallest obstruction.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact reconstruction theorem</summary>

The genuine complex equals the flag completion of its pairwise graph if and only if every graph clique is contained in one common document. This property is called **conformality**. Read more about [simplicial complexes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simplicial_complex) and [hypergraphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypergraph).

</details>

## Construct the witness-sensitive complex

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

## Diagnose pairwise information loss

The next procedure returns maximal cliques that no single document witnesses. Each result is a concrete certificate of nonconformality.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Why maximal cliques suffice</summary>

Every finite clique lies inside a maximal clique. If each maximal clique has a document witness, that witness also contains every subclique. Thus checking maximal cliques checks all cliques.

</details>

## Count what topology could possibly contain

The number of $k$-faces is at most $ inom{n}{k+1}$, and homology cannot exceed its chain space. Explore both the sharp binomial ceiling and the coarse $n^{k+1}$ ceiling.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the dimension obstruction</summary>

A $k$-simplex needs $k+1$ distinct vertices. On $n$ vertices, no such simplex exists for $k\ge n$, so $ eta_k=0$. This rules out a positive power law uniform in dimension. Background: [Betti numbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Betti_number).

</details>

## Compute homology from boundary ranks

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

## Follow topology through time

Nested corpora generate nested complexes, the basic input for [persistent homology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistent_homology).

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Interpretation guide</summary>

A persistent loop is a durable incidence pattern, not intrinsically a named research community. A persistent cavity is a durable missing higher-order witness, not intrinsically a paradigm shift. Semantic conclusions require metadata or a model connecting labels to topology.

</details>

